Profound intellectual and multiple disabilities
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
IQ < 20, Total dependence for ADLs, Severe motor/mobility impairments, Absent/restricted verbal communication

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-041
Reason for Visit: Acute enteral feeding tube blockage and suspected micro-aspiration.
Location: Group residential home
Present at Visit: Patient and two support workers
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a blocked percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) tube preventing essential hydration and medication administration for the past 8 hours, alongside a new-onset wet cough. The assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period due to the high risk of rapidly progressive dehydration, missed critical anti-epileptic medication, and the immediate need to exclude aspiration pneumonia in a highly vulnerable patient.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: We cannot push any fluids or his seizure medications through the tube, and he started a wet, gurgling cough an hour ago. (Reported by support worker)
History of Present Illness: The support workers report an 8-hour history of total PEG tube occlusion in a patient with profound cognitive impairment and absent/severely restricted verbal communication abilities. The patient is totally dependent on caregivers for all activities of daily living and has severe motor impairments resulting in complete lack of independent mobility (wheelchair reliance). Earlier today, thick enteral formula was administered without adequate water flushing. For the past hour, the patient has exhibited a continuous wet cough and mild tachypnoea, raising high clinical suspicion for aspiration of oral secretions due to inability to manage saliva while distressed.
Functional Status: Bedbound/wheelchair dependent; requires 24-hour total nursing care.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of intractable seizures due to missed regular anticonvulsant dosing.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 mmHg | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 37.3 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 45.0 Kg
General: Patient is awake, exhibiting non-purposeful movements, and frequently coughing with pooled oral secretions.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination confirms profound intellectual disability (historical IQ < 20) and severe spastic quadriplegia with joint contractures. Respiratory examination reveals transmitted upper airway sounds and diminished air entry at the right lung base, but no focal coarse crackles. Abdominal examination reveals a 20 Fr PEG tube with a visible, solid occlusion in the external lumen; the stoma site is clean, non-erythematous, and without purulent discharge.
Environmental Observations: Suction equipment is present at the bedside but the canister is unemptied.
Medication Reconciliation: Missed evening dose of Sodium Valproate and Levetiracetam due to tube blockage.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Profound intellectual and multiple disabilities (complicated by acute PEG occlusion and upper airway secretion pooling) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Lower Respiratory Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of focal lung crackles, absence of high fever, and SpO2 maintaining >92% on room air.

PEG Site Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of erythema, induration, warmth, or purulent discharge at the stoma site.

Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to a soft, non-distended abdomen, present bowel sounds, and a reported normal bowel movement this morning.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of a blocked enteral access device and emerging respiratory distress required immediate in-person evaluation. The objective findings of a visibly occluded PEG tube and resting tachypnoea confirmed that the patient was entirely cut off from life-sustaining hydration and essential anticonvulsant medications. This assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period because failure to immediately unblock the tube and administer the missed anti-epileptics would rapidly precipitate status epilepticus and severe dehydration, necessitating an avoidable emergency department admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Successfully unblocked the PEG tube using warm water instillation, gentle negative pressure via a 50mL syringe, and manual massage of the external tubing. Administered the missed doses of liquid anticonvulsants immediately. Performed oropharyngeal suctioning to clear pooled secretions.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you implement a strict 30mL warm water flush immediately before and after every single feed or medication dose, you prevent formula from solidifying; therefore, completing this specific flushing routine guarantees the tube remains patent. When you empty and test the suction canister at the beginning of every shift, the equipment is ready instantly; establishing this daily habit ensures you can rapidly clear his airway when he coughs.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to the community dietetics team to review the viscosity of the current enteral formula.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ordered a replacement PEG tube to keep on-site in case of future irreversible mechanical failure.
Follow-up: Routine GP review tomorrow to assess respiratory status and confirm ongoing tube patency.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops a fever over 38.0 C, if the tube falls out completely, or if a seizure occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The unblocking procedure is a temporary mechanical fix; recurrent blockages indicate a need to alter the feeding regimen.
Clinical Course: Hydration and medication delivery are restored. The mild cough is expected to resolve with consistent oral suctioning and keeping the patient elevated at 45 degrees.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Ensure the patient remains sitting upright for at least one hour after every feed. Seek urgent medical care if his breathing becomes rapid, shallow, or labored.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The thick liquid food dried like cement inside the tube because it was not flushed with enough water. The warm water and pressure dissolved the blockage safely. He was coughing because his normal saliva was pooling in his throat while he was lying flat and distressed.
Actionable Steps: Flush the tube with 30mL of warm water before and after every use. Keep the head of the bed elevated to 45 degrees. Administer all medications via the unblocked tube immediately.

